  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "collections arrays list dictionary set stack queue concurrent and immutable collections",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "A collection packages a storage shape and a set of operations. Pick it from the access and update pattern, ordering rules, key semantics, concurrency requirements, and lifetime of the data rather than from its familiar name.",
    "Arrays provide fixed-length indexed storage; List<T> is a resizable indexed sequence; dictionaries map keys to values; sets enforce uniqueness; queues and stacks express FIFO and LIFO access. Sorted collections pay extra work to preserve order.",
    "Interfaces such as IEnumerable<T>, IReadOnlyCollection<T>, IList<T>, and IDictionary<TKey,TValue> describe capabilities and can decouple code from storage choices. Read-only interfaces do not guarantee immutable backing data.",
    "Most System.Collections.Generic collections are not synchronized for concurrent mutation. Use concurrent collections for supported atomic operations or protect ordinary collections with a lock when a compound invariant spans operations.",
    "Immutable collections return updated values while preserving previous versions. Frozen collections are built for read-heavy lookup after construction. These structures trade update cost, allocation, and lookup behavior differently; benchmark the actual workload."
  ],
